            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              <div id="" class="zn-text "   ><p>I don’t like talking about my feelings. Heck, as an introverted writer who lives deep in the mountains of Western North Carolina, I don’t much like talking at all. But earlier this year, as the lingering trauma of Hurricane Helene mixed with the nail-biting uncertainty of our country’s future, I found myself on a psychotherapist’s sofa, wading through messy emotions. </p><p><br></p><p>Two big things I learned: First, we all needed to be hugged more as children. (Seriously, parents, hug your kids.) Second, we could all benefit from wildflower hikes.       </p><p><br></p><p>As I was spiraling about egg prices, political polarization, and raging forest fires, my counselor reminded me to search for beauty in the world. “Get outside,” she said. “Walk mindfully, and look for the wildflowers. Pay attention to their leaves and their blooms. Learn their names. Know their stories.”</p></div>
            </div>
            
            
          </div>
        

